+---
+description: "Backward-compatibility shim serving legacy service faces (apiProxy) and client module aliases to pre-gateway dsh plugins, for deployers of old plugins and maintainers retiring them."
+kind: "package-reference"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-compat-shim
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+`dsh-compat-shim` keeps old dsh plugins running against the current harness: a host-side service re-exposes the removed `apiProxy` RPC face over the api-gateway Remote services, and the client half pairs with a `seed.ts` module alias so browser plugins that import the removed `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-runtime/client` keep resolving. It is a deployment-local compatibility layer, not a product capability: mount it while old plugins need it, and retire it when the last consumer migrates.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+
+-----
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+Mount it as a bundle on the HOST plane: the `dsh.bundle.patch` inserts one row (`compat-shim`) that provides the `apiProxy` service, and the `dsh.client` declaration serves the browser half. Old plugins resolve `ctx.apiProxy` exactly as they did before the api-gateway migration; the shim forwards each call to the corresponding Remote service and wraps the result in the old `{ rpcId, result: { ok, value | error } }` envelope.
+
+### When to choose it
+
+Choose it when a deployment still runs plugins built against the removed `apiProxy` or `dsh-client-runtime` faces. Skip it once every consumer has migrated — each forwarding entry is a promise to keep answering an old shape, and the shim should shrink to zero.
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+`createApiProxy` closes over the host context and forwards session, workspace, agent-preset, and host-describe calls to `ctx.remote.*`, translating business rejections into the legacy error envelope; `downloads.sessionLog` is a stub that throws. The client plugin is a marker: the actual browser alias (`dsh-client-runtime/client` → `dsh-client-store`) is installed by the web client's static module table (`seed.ts`), so the alias cannot drift between the two files.
+
+## Model Experience
+
+Indirectly, through the old plugins it keeps alive: the shim itself registers no prompt section, tool schema, or model context, and every model-facing effect belongs to the Remote services (or the browser plugins) it forwards to.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+Independent of every request: the shim contributes no tokens to any model request and therefore cannot invalidate a reusable prefix.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **The bridge is untyped** — the legacy RPC request/response shapes are plain JS-in-TS; oxlint's unsafe-any rules are disabled for `packages/compat/**` under an explicit override, and each face should gain real types as its last consumer migrates off.
+- **`conversationEvents` is announced, not provided** — the package header names it, but `apply` only publishes `apiProxy`; old plugins depending on that face still break until it is added.
+- **`downloads.sessionLog` is a stub** — the legacy download call throws instead of streaming a session log; callers must treat it as unsupported.
+- **Outside the aggregate typecheck** — the package is not referenced from `tsconfig.host.json`, so `pnpm run build` never recompiles it; its `lib/` is built by hand and the profile install carries its own copy.
