+---
+description: "The model-facing enter_plan_mode tool: the agent proposes switching its session into plan mode, while plan state and the exit review stay owned by dsh-plan-mode."
+kind: "package-reference"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-plan-mode
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+`dsh-tool-plan-mode` gives the agent one tool, `enter_plan_mode`, to propose switching its session into plan mode before starting non-trivial implementation work. It completes the plan-mode loop for autonomous agents: without it, only the user can turn plan mode on, so an agent that decides mid-task that the user should approve the approach first has no way to say so. Plan state, the plan-policy prompt section, the `/plan` command, and the reviewed `exit_plan_mode` transition stay owned by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode`; this package only forwards the model's intent to that controller and reports the outcome.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+
+-----
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+Mount it beside `@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode` in the same scope — an agent preset's planning group, or a host composition that runs the controller globally. The plugin declares `['tools', 'planMode']` as its injects; a composition without the controller fails at inject resolution rather than degrading silently. The tool takes no configuration.
+
+### When to choose it
+
+Choose it when the agent should decide for itself when a task needs an approved plan — autonomous or long-horizon presets, where no user is watching to flip the mode. Skip it where the workflow already fixes who plans: short automation runs, or deployments that treat plan mode as a purely user-driven switch.
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+`apply` reads the `planMode` controller once and registers one tool. The execute path rejects calls while plan mode is already active, then calls `ctx.planMode.set(agent, true)` and maps the outcome: a tool call always runs inside an open turn, so the normal result is a queued selection that lands at the next accepted pre-step; `noop` with an inactive log means an identical selection is already queued, and `cancelled` clears an opposite pending selection because the log already reads active. Leaving plan mode is untouched: `exit_plan_mode` keeps its user review, so entry is the only model-initiated transition and it only ever restricts the agent.
+
+The mode-change narration the controller injects at the step boundary is source-neutral ("Plan mode is now active"), because the user (a `/plan` selection) and the agent (this tool) both land there.
+
+## Model Experience
+
+### Request context and condition
+
+#### What the model sees
+
+The generated [`enter_plan_mode` schema](../../../docs/tool-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-tool-plan-mode): a parameterless tool whose description states when to propose plan mode and that `/plan off` cancels or leaves it. The result is a fixed two-field object (`entered`, `queued`) rendered as one sentence that names the next step — ground the plan in inspection, then present it with `exit_plan_mode`. A call while plan mode is already active fails with an error naming `exit_plan_mode` instead.
+
+#### Token effect
+
+Fixed, small: one tool schema plus the one-sentence result per call. The controller's plan-policy section appears only while plan mode is active and is owned by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode`.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+The tool schema is a stable prefix member of every request for sessions on a composition that mounts it, including sessions that never call it. Results append; nothing replaces earlier request tokens. A queued selection does not alter the tool catalog — the exit tool stays registered in both modes for the same reason.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **No consent round-trip on entry** — a model-initiated switch commits (or queues) without asking the user first, mirroring `/plan`; the restrictiveness of plan mode is the safety property, and leaving still requires the user's review. A consent-gated variant would need an approval-policy surface this package does not own.
+- **In-turn entry waits for the step boundary** — like every plan selection made inside an open turn, the switch applies at the next accepted pre-step; the tool result says so in model-visible text rather than hiding the delay.
