@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+---
+description: "Cross-session scheduled automations: durable stored prompts that spawn a fresh session when due, for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging the capability."
+kind: "package-reference"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-automations
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+`dsh-automations` gives an agent durable, cross-session scheduling: it stores a titled prompt together with a schedule and, when the schedule comes due, spawns a FRESH session over the recorded workspace and agent preset and delivers the prompt as an ordinary user message. Reminders (`dsh-schedule`) arrive inside the conversation that asked for them; automations outlive that conversation entirely — the creating session can end, the process can restart, and the next due fire still opens its own session. Three model-facing tools (`automation_create`, `automation_list`, `automation_cancel`) cover the lifecycle.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+
+-----
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+Mount it on the HOST plane with the storage stack: it injects `tools` and `storageDomain`, persists records in the `automations` storage domain, and owns one process-level due driver. The config carries three required bounds — `maxPromptBytes`, `minDelaySeconds`, `minIntervalSeconds` — a composition that omits them fails at load.
+
+### When to choose it
+
+Choose it when work should happen at a time, not in a conversation: nightly sweeps, delayed follow-ups, fixed-rate checks — each running unattended in its own session. Skip it when the outcome should land back into the current conversation; that is `dsh-schedule`'s contract.
+
+### Minimal configuration
+
+```yaml
+- id: automations
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-automations'
+  config:
+    maxPromptBytes: 65536
+    minDelaySeconds: 60
+    minIntervalSeconds: 60
+```
+
+`after` and `at` schedules are one-shot; `every` is fixed-rate recurring with an optional `maxRuns` ceiling. The creating agent anchors the record: its session cwd becomes the fired session's workspace and its composed preset is re-mounted at fire time, so a fire replays the creating environment without anyone present.
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+`AutomationsService` opens the storage domain in its init, registers the three tools on the host tool layer, and starts the due driver. The driver is one re-armed timer: each wake fires every record due at that moment (a failed one-shot is retained with its `lastError` and never re-fires; a recurring record re-arms from its anchor so missed occurrences coalesce into one catch-up fire), then schedules the next wake. Firing composes the session through `ctx.agents.create` with the recorded `cwd` and preset — the same creation path the webhook ingress uses — attaches the workspace and title when those services are composed, and delivers the prompt behind a one-sentence framing naming the automation. Spawn-time services (`agents`, `agentPresets`, optional `workspaceRegistry`/`sessionTitle`) resolve at fire time and fail loud there, because registration never needs them.
+
+## Model Experience
+
+### Request context and condition
+
+#### What the model sees
+
+The generated [`automation_create` / `automation_list` / `automation_cancel` schemas](../../../docs/tool-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-automations): create takes a title, a self-contained prompt, one of three schedule kinds, and an optional recurring ceiling; list renders one line per automation with next fire time, fire count, and the last failure; cancel takes an id. A fire is invisible to the creating session — it is a user message in the NEW session, prefixed by the automation framing.
+
+#### Token effect
+
+Fixed, small: three tool schemas in every request of every session on the deployment, plus the one-line list render per call.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+The three schemas are a stable prefix member of every request; list/cancel results append. A fired automation does not touch the creating session's log at all, so scheduling never invalidates an existing conversation's reusable prefix.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **The process must be alive at the due time** — the driver is a process timer; occurrences missed while the host was down coalesce into one fire at the next tick or boot, and a one-shot whose moment passed while nothing ran fires late rather than being skipped.
+- **No cron expressions** — schedules are `after`/`at`/`every` like `dsh-schedule`; cron semantics would need a maintained parser and are deferred until a deployment asks.
+- **Fires are unreviewed** — a due automation spawns a session without a consent round-trip; the stored prompt was fixed at creation time, and the session runs under the host's default approval stack. A consent gate would need an approval-policy surface this package does not own.
